<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=aeroplane">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Transport and navigation</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aeroplane</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1903">1903</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Airplane</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">An aeroplane is a fixed-wing aircraft driven forward by the thrust of a propeller, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jet-engine">jet engine</a> or rocket engine; the Wright brothers invented and flew the Wright Flyer in 1903, in what is recognised as the first sustained and controlled heavier-than-air powered flight. They built on George Cayley's concept of 1799 and on the gliding flights of Otto Lilienthal. Aeroplanes served in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> and in every major battle of the Second, the first jet aircraft flew in 1939, and commercial aviation now carries more than four billion passengers a year.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aeroplane.jpg" alt="Aeroplane" width="320" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jules Beau · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:14-bis_de_Alberto_Santos_Dumont.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1903</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>United States</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Wright brothers · <a href="/empire/united-states">United States</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Clément Ader’s Éole made an uncontrolled hop in 1890, and Alberto Santos-Dumont’s flight of 1906 was claimed as the first without a catapult.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/internal-combustion-engine">Internal combustion engine<span>1794</span></a></div><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Led to</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/jet-engine">Jet engine<span>1937</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=aeroplane">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
